## Tooling & IDE Support

| Tool | What It Helps With |
|------|--------------------|
| **SpotBugs / FindBugs** | Detects improper lambda usage (e.g., capturing non‑final variables). |
| **Checkstyle / PMD** | Enforces lambda style guidelines, flags long lambda bodies. |
| **SonarQube** | Detects side‑effects in streams, checks for performance issues. |
| **IntelliJ IDEA / Eclipse** | Provides quick-fixes for method reference ambiguity, adds `@FunctionalInterface`. |
| **JMH** | Benchmarking to decide whether a lambda is worth it. |

---

## Bottom Line

Lambda expressions are a powerful addition to Java’s language, but they come with a **new set of semantics** that differ from traditional anonymous inner classes:

| Anonymous Inner Class | Lambda |
|------------------------|--------|
| Must be written as a **full class body** | Can be written as a single expression or block |
| Captures variables **by reference** | Captures variables **by value** (effectively final) |
| Allows `break`/`continue` inside loops | Cannot use `break`/`continue` |
| Can throw any exception | Only unchecked exceptions (unless wrapped) |
| Verbose | Concise |

When you keep the checklist above in mind, the risk of subtle bugs, readability problems, or performance regressions drops dramatically.  

Happy lambda‑coding!
